te horizon sum of discounted  rewards from starting at state s naught  taking action a and transitioning  according to the dynamics of the  environment  and so the first problem is that  typically in reinforcement learning data  or data sets from the real world there  is a temporal dependence in this data  set and temporally dependent data slows  down learning because subsequent  observations that you see are very close  to each other  and so one way that people have  developed and practice to deal with this  is to use an experience replay to break  temporal dependencies and to speed up  learning so to experience replay works  in the following way we save transition  state action reward next state into a  buffer and then we sample from this  buffer and we use the samples from this  buffer to train the agent so that  subsequent observations that the agent  sees are not going to be as temporarily  dependent they're going to be closer to  an iid case  and so we study experience replay in the  context of least squares regression so  we have our observations y equals to x  transpose w star w star is the learnable  parameter and x is uh markovian data so  our features x come from a markov chain  which is the auto regressive chain which  is a sort of like a random walk  and we want to minimize the out of  population loss of a sample from the  stationary distribution so that's our  loss  and so our main result is that sgd with  experience replay for the auto  aggressive chain can speed up the  learning so we show an upper bound for  sgd with experience replay uh that where  the loss decays exponentially and the  number of time steps over the square  root of the mixing time and we show a  lower round for sequential sgd so  without experience replay where the loss  decays exponentially and in the number  of iterations over the mixing time so we  plot the log loss on the right we see  that uh the loss for sgd with experience  replay is bounded in the green region  and the loss for sequential sgd is in  the red region as a function of the  number of iterations t this is one of  the first analyses for experience replay  oh okay and the second um  so the second uh  main challenge in reinforcement learning  is the issue of distribution shift  between the training data and what the  real world data and this is particularly  relevant in the case of offline  reinforcement learning so in many real  world scenarios the deployment of a  poorly understood policy for the  purposes of online learning can be too  costly or too risky so we must rely only  on the offline data set to find a  reasonable policy for deployment  and so say that we have a fixed data set  of trajectories collected by some  behavioral policy  s not  a r that is not coming from the policy  that you're actually interested in  understanding  so there is a distribution shift between  these two data sets  and we also want to look at the problem  of off policy evaluation which arises in  main two main contexts so the first is a  ranking problem given a set of potential  target policies that you're interested  in for deployment pi one through pi n  evaluate them and pick the best one to  deploy could be the highest value policy  or the safest policy and the second  situation is for offline learning so as  a subroutine for offline learning agents  need to iterate towards better policies  based on accurate value estimates from  off policy  evaluation and so we mainly focus on the  problem of ranking  and one main one very popular algorithm  for of policy evaluation is fitted q  evaluation and  in terms of accuracy recent papers have  shown that it achieves state-of-the-art  accuracy however there is also work out  there that talks about the stability of  fqe in particular it can diverge to plus  or negative infinity for both  linear and non-linear function  approximation  so we wanted to understand how  architecture and representation affects  stability and accuracy for fqe and  variant