er delta 1 over  epsilon and this is nearly linear this  is a nearly linear time nearly linearly  convergent algorithms for solving  discounted mdps and we have a second  algorithm that computes an epsilon  optimal policy with high probability and  sample complexity and runtime that is  cubic and one over one minus gamma and  this is nearly optimal up to log factors  and sample and runtime complexity using  only the generative model  and so in conclusion my research or my  goals are to build solutions that are  simple easy to understand explain and  engineer effective that they work well  in practice and also are theoretically  well-rounded thank you  thanks karen  got some questions  maybe tell us a little bit about where  the  logarithmic dependence on epsilon  arrives in the um  uh  the  positive result about  with the generative model  uh yeah it was it's a variance reduced  value iteration method so  i don't i don't know how well the  audience is familiar with value  iteration but it's a successive  approximation method um  basically you uh you you like solve  for it's an iterative method right you  solve you start off with a value vector  and then you  uh you and then you use the bellman  updates uh and then you're getting  closer and closer to the  true uh value function via a fixed point  uh contraction mapping properties of  the bellman operator and so basically  our uh and so  when you use that with a generative  model basically when you have a  generative model you use the simulator  to produce samples of s prime so  basically the algorithm will query from  the simulator to get uh samples of s  prime from the from the uh like  according to the real transition  probabilities and so instead of having  uh like exact value iteration uh uh  exact value iterations you have like  approximate value iterations and then so  our variance reduced  method on top of that it's very simple  and it's inspired by like various  variants reduced sgd that's already out  there